      <p>Brent reached the dataset maximum of <strong>$130.00/barrel</strong> in the week of March 28, 2022, following the Russian invasion of Ukraine (Feb 24, 2022). Global petrol averaged <strong>$2.78/liter</strong> at the peak — a <strong>173% increase</strong> over the April 2020 trough. The $130 ceiling persisted for 13 consecutive weeks (March–June 2022).</p>
    </div>
    <div class="insight-box">
      <h4>Recovery &amp; Structural Shift (2023–2026)</h4>
      <p>2023 saw a mild correction (annual avg: $2.23/liter petrol, Brent $104.43). But 2024–2026 resumed an upward path, with 2026 YTD establishing new highs. The post-2022 floor appears structurally higher than pre-2021 levels — suggesting permanent demand-supply rebalancing.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="insight-box success">
      <h4>Volatility Pattern</h4>
      <p>Cross-sectional standard deviation in petrol prices peaked in <strong>2022 ($1.63)</strong>, reflecting the wide divergence between high-subsidy (price-insulated) and low-subsidy (fully exposed) countries during the energy crisis. The 2020 shock had lower std dev ($1.01) as even unsubsidized markets saw moderated declines from pre-existing price floors.</p>
    </div>
