<!-- ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════ SECTION 6: KEY FINDINGS -->
<div class="section">
  <div class="section-header">
    <span class="section-number">Section 06</span>
    <h2>Key Findings &amp; Policy Implications</h2>
  </div>
  <p class="section-objective">Synthesizes the most important insights from the analysis into actionable findings, with recommendations for policymakers on subsidy design, tax calibration, and price stabilization mechanisms.</p>

  <ul class="findings-list">
    <li>
      <div class="finding-num">1</div>
      <div class="finding-body">
        <div class="tag">Core Finding</div>
        <h4>Brent Crude Is the Dominant Price Driver — Structural Pass-Through Is Near-Total in Unsubsidized Markets</h4>
        <p>The Pearson correlation between weekly Brent Crude and global-average retail prices is r = 1.00, confirming that unsubsidized retail fuel prices move in near-perfect lockstep with crude benchmarks. Between April 2020 and March 2022, Brent rose 171% and global average petrol rose 172%. Policymakers in low-subsidy regimes cannot meaningfully shield consumers from commodity shocks without fiscal intervention.</p>
      </div>
    </li>
    <li>
      <div class="finding-num">2</div>
      <div class="finding-body">
        <div class="tag policy">Policy Implication</div>
        <h4>Subsidies Provide Powerful Price Dampening — But at Highly Variable Fiscal Efficiency</h4>
        <p>The 79.5% subsidy dampening effect is striking but misleading as a policy benchmark. Very-High-subsidy countries (Venezuela, Iran, Iraq, Libya, Algeria) achieve $0.01–$0.16/liter prices through direct fiscal transfers or administered pricing that can amount to 10–20% of GDP in subsidy cost. High-subsidy countries (e.g., GCC states) benefit from domestic crude ownership that reduces fiscal cost. Middle-income countries face the worst trade-off: large subsidy bills without comparable resource wealth. Policymakers should assess subsidy cost-efficiency by income level rather than treating subsidy as a binary good.</p>
      </div>
    </li>
    <li>
      <div class="finding-num">3</div>
      <div class="finding-body">
        <div class="tag">Core Finding</div>
        <h4>The 2022 Energy Crisis Was the Single Largest Consumer Shock — and Its Structural Effects Persist</h4>
        <p>Brent hit $130/barrel in March 2022 and stayed at that ceiling for 13 weeks. The post-2022 global petrol average has not returned to 2020 levels and 2026 YTD ($2.65/liter) exceeds the 2022 annual average ($2.51). This persistent re-basing suggests that pre-2021 prices are unlikely to return — policymakers should calibrate subsidy and stabilization programs against this new higher baseline rather than historical averages.</p>
      </div>
    </li>
    <li>
      <div class="finding-num">4</div>
      <div class="finding-body">
        <div class="tag risk">Risk Factor</div>
        <h4>Europe and Oceania Face the Highest Absolute Exposure — and Declining Policy Room</h4>
        <p>European petrol averaged $4.06/liter at the 2022 crisis peak — the highest regional price in any year. With existing tax rates of 30–50% of pump price, governments face limited downside tax-reduction headroom without foregoing major revenue. The 2022 EU windfall profit tax on energy companies was a one-time measure; systematic stabilization mechanisms (fuel price floors/ceilings, strategic reserve releases) require institutional design before the next shock.</p>
      </div>
    </li>
    <li>
      <div class="finding-num">5</div>
      <div class="finding-body">
        <div class="tag equity">Equity Concern</div>
        <h4>Fuel Affordability Is an Equity Crisis in Low-Income Countries — Disproportionate Tax Burden Compounds the Problem</h4>
        <p>Low-income countries pay $1.39/liter average petrol (vs. $3.55 in high-income) but face a 22.9% average tax rate — higher than middle-income countries (21.1%). At the same time, they lack the subsidy depth of very-high-subsidy petrostates. The 2020–2026 price increase of 79% in low-income markets represents a severe and compounding affordability stress that standard economic analysis (focused on absolute price levels) underestimates. Targeted cash transfer mechanisms or LPG subsidies may offer more fiscally efficient protection than generalized petrol subsidies.</p>
      </div>
    </li>
    <li>
      <div class="finding-num">6</div>
      <div class="finding-body">
        <div class="tag policy">Policy Implication</div>
        <h4>Regional Spread is Widening — Inter-Country Policy Divergence Is Accelerating</h4>
        <p>The spread between the highest (Europe: $4.29) and lowest (Middle East: $1.42) regional average petrol prices in 2026 is $2.87/liter — up 78% from the 2020 spread of $1.61. This widening reflects divergent subsidy and tax trajectories. As crude prices remain elevated, subsidy-maintaining governments will face ever-larger fiscal deficits, while tax-heavy governments will face political pressure to cut rates. Policy coordination at the G20/IEA level on subsidy reform timelines and stabilization fund design would reduce global price inequality.</p>
      </div>
    </li>
    <li>
      <div class="finding-num">7</div>
      <div class="finding-body">
        <div class="tag policy">Policy Recommendation</div>
        <h4>Graduated Subsidy Reform Tied to Income-Targeted Transfers Is the Optimal Transition Path</h4>
        <p>The data strongly support a graduated subsidy removal strategy: maintain very-high subsidy regimes only where domestic crude production makes them fiscally self-sustaining (GCC); implement targeted cash transfers to replace blanket petrol subsidies in middle- and low-income countries (the Indonesia/Egypt model); use the price gap between LPG ($1.71 global avg) and petrol ($2.28) to prioritize LPG transition support in low-income markets where cooking/transport substitution is feasible; and design automatic price stabilization corridors (e.g., consumer price held to ±20% of 3-year rolling average Brent-equivalent) to limit future volatility exposure without permanent fiscal commitment.</p>
      </div>
    </li>
  </ul>
</div>

